The first element refusal is data: gendrake.ElementRefusalOf returns a kind with no keyword as a Problem, over the walk RefusalOf now makes (GitHub #11)

OWNING ISSUE: GitHub #11 (open; stays open). Nothing on the tracker was touched.

WHY. After ed37e5d the codec refusals were a definition's and four element refusals stayed hand-written in the emitter's
`refuseElement`, walked over the definition by `refuseDefinition`: a kind with no keyword, invalid method formals, a value
outside the tree language, a block carrying a value outside its body. All four share the walk — which elements the surface
renders, and the recursion into each element's parameters and body. Dev accepted "Accepted, build it" on moving the walk and
the smallest refusal, the kind with no keyword, into definitions, the other three to follow as points of their own.

WHAT.
- New trio `gendrake.ElementRefusalOf`: factory `par element TypeElement`, `par subject TypeName`, one element
  `problems [Problem]`. A kind with no row in `draketarget.Keyword.table` (Action, Factory, Pattern: sections, never member
  lines) is one Problem with the emitter's message, unchanged; then the element's parameters and body, recursively. Authored
  in drake with its `uses` record, JSON `DrakeCLI parse`, Scala `GeneratorCLI generate`; listed by GenDrake.
- `gendrake.RefusalOf` gains the walk and two elements: `codecProblems` (the three refusals of ed37e5d), `elementProblems`
  (ElementRefusalOf over the type section's elements, a PRESENT factory's parameters and body, the globals, a present
  rule's variables, conditions and action, a present actor's three blocks — the elements `refuseDefinition` walks, in its
  order) and `problems`, the two joined, codec first. An absent factory's or role's elements are not judged, as before.
- Drake.scala: `refuseDefinition` raises the first of `RefusalOf(td).elementProblems` before the three refusals it still
  judges by hand; `emit` keeps raising the first of `problems`; `bareKeyword` is removed and `refuseElement` no longer calls it.
- The §6 generation order was followed (ElementRefusalOf generated and compiled, then RefusalOf and GenDrake); all three
  trios stable on the recompiled engine.
- ElementRefusalOfTest (6), the 93rd suite: keyed kinds at any depth; each unkeyed kind with its message and subject; the
  recursion in order; the walk (present and absent factory, globals, rule, actor, codec first); the element read once and
  lazily; the live emitter and its three section entry points raising the first problem, the hand-judged refusals after it.

ONE DIFFERENCE, as recommended. The keyword refusal is judged over the whole definition before the other three, so a
definition malformed in two ways may raise a different first message (the new suite pins the case); and it is raised as an
IllegalArgumentException where it was a RuntimeException (still one, by subclass). No existing test changed.

